		'word' => 'SINCE 2011',
		'sub' => 'a school built on a spoon',
		'img' => 'v1-school.webp',
		'alt' => 'The school building on the canal.',
		'pull' => 'Founded in 2011, Saji Culinary School was created to teach classical culinary craft through real restaurant service. Our teaching kitchens occupy a former ship\'s-chandler warehouse along Rotterdam\'s historic Veerkade canal.',
		'paras' => array(  ),
		'go' => array( '/instructors/', 'Meet the instructors' ),
		'sections' => array(
			array( 'Our founding on the Veerkade', 'Saji Culinary School was founded in 2011 by chef Marta Ilić and baker Owen Achterberg. Seeking an ideal environment for serious culinary instruction, they renovated a nineteenth-century brick-and-timber ship\'s-chandler warehouse at Veerkade 18, along Rotterdam\'s historic canal. They installed heavy stainless steel stoves, walk-in cold rooms, deck baking ovens, and a dedicated service pass. Their objective was simple: build an independent teaching kitchen focused strictly on foundational technique, daily repetition, and commercial kitchen discipline.' ),
			array( 'The tradition of the tasting spoon', 'The school takes its name from the Japanese word saji, meaning tasting spoon. On their first morning at Veerkade 18, every student is issued a simple stainless steel tasting spoon alongside their knife roll. This spoon remains in the sleeve pocket of every cook throughout forty weeks of study. Instructors insist that nothing leaves a station without being tasted, evaluated, and adjusted for temperature, acidity, and seasoning. The spoon represents mindfulness, humility, and constant sensory accountability.' ),
			array( 'Cooking for real guests from week three', 'Traditional culinary academies often restrict student cooking to simulated practicals and isolated classrooms. At Saji, students begin cooking for real paying guests at The Pass starting in week three of their diploma. Stepping up to the pass under service tickets teaches students the realities of ticket timing, customer expectations, and kitchen urgency far more effectively than theoretical lectures. Cooking for the public instils pride, resilience, and immediate respect for the dining guest.' ),
			array( 'Why does the school bear the name Saji?', 'The name saji honors the tasting spoon, the most fundamental yet overlooked tool in the professional kitchen. While knives shape ingredients and flames cook them, the tasting spoon determines whether food is truly fit to serve. Carrying this small steel tool reminds cooks that technique exists solely to produce delicious, nourishing food. It symbolizes our commitment to palate development, continuous attention to detail, and humility before the craft of cooking.' ),
		),
